{
  "dom_id": "step-q71361",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5},\\,\\sqrt[3]{50},\\,\\sqrt[3]{125}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{},$ cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{},$ $\\sqrt[5]{},…,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is a surd because $5$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{5}=1.709\\,975\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ is a surd because $50$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{50}=3.684\\,031\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{125}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $125$ is a perfect cube, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{})$ and obtain a whole number.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{125} & =\\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
